WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.701
- [Narrator] In 1904, St. Louis
hosted an Olympic marathon

00:00:03.701 --> 00:00:05.071
for the ages.

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:07.184
Overshadowed by the
neighboring World's Fair,

00:00:07.184 --> 00:00:08.833
the poorly-planned event was reduced

00:00:08.833 --> 00:00:12.629
to a lackluster sideshow
primed for disaster.

00:00:12.629 --> 00:00:14.487
During a sweltering 90-degree day,

00:00:14.487 --> 00:00:16.913
the runners were thrust
onto a hellish route

00:00:16.913 --> 00:00:20.010
that cut through car
traffic, rocks, thick dust,

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:21.593
and 300-foot hills.

00:00:22.727 --> 00:00:24.782
Of the 32 athletes who competed,

00:00:24.782 --> 00:00:26.279
only 14 finished.

00:00:26.279 --> 00:00:30.446
And of the 14, these are the
three worth knowing about.

00:00:34.557 --> 00:00:36.379
Fred Lorz was a fresh-faced athlete

00:00:36.379 --> 00:00:38.434
ready to make a name for himself.

00:00:38.434 --> 00:00:40.570
For nine miles, Lorz was
near the front of the pack,

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:43.775
until painful muscle cramps
stopped him in his tracks.

00:00:43.775 --> 00:00:45.273
And, he wasn't the only one.

00:00:45.273 --> 00:00:47.490
Almost every single racer
had severe muscle cramps

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:49.237
that day, thanks to this dummy.

00:00:49.237 --> 00:00:51.408
Chief event organizer
and amateur scientist,

00:00:51.408 --> 00:00:52.984
James Sullivan.

00:00:52.984 --> 00:00:55.573
You see, Sullivan turned
the event into an experiment

00:00:55.573 --> 00:00:59.369
to grasp how purposeful
dehydration affects athletes.

00:00:59.369 --> 00:01:02.284
Immune to common sense, Sullivan
put a single water station

00:01:02.284 --> 00:01:04.216
at the 11th mile mark.

00:01:04.216 --> 00:01:08.512
So, for 26 miles, the runners
relied on one water station

00:01:08.512 --> 00:01:10.044
that was dirty.

00:01:10.044 --> 00:01:12.157
And gave you dysentery.

00:01:12.157 --> 00:01:13.969
James Sullivan, what a guy.

00:01:13.969 --> 00:01:14.802
Oops.

00:01:14.802 --> 00:01:16.102
Anyway, back to Lorz.

00:01:16.102 --> 00:01:19.190
With he sun beating down,
road dust clogging his lungs,

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:22.034
and his muscles spazzing in
protest, Lorz had enough,

00:01:22.034 --> 00:01:24.705
and traded in his two
legs for four wheels.

00:01:24.705 --> 00:01:26.922
He hitched a car ride
to the 19th mile mark,

00:01:26.922 --> 00:01:29.802
got out, and gingerly ran
through the finish line.

00:01:29.802 --> 00:01:31.891
The crowd went wild, but
before the gold medal

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:36.058
could be placed around his
neck, his ruse was discovered.

00:01:37.035 --> 00:01:39.888
Next up, Andarín Carvajal.

00:01:39.888 --> 00:01:41.971
He was a five-foot-tall Cuban national,

00:01:41.971 --> 00:01:43.945
and former mailman turned athlete.

00:01:43.945 --> 00:01:46.499
To get to St. Louis,
Carvajal had to raise money

00:01:46.499 --> 00:01:48.032
by competing in running events,

00:01:48.032 --> 00:01:51.619
including a 700-mile race across Cuba.

00:01:51.619 --> 00:01:54.417
Once he got to St. Louis,
he was easy to spot.

00:01:54.417 --> 00:01:56.844
Among the sleeveless shirts
and thigh-high shorts,

00:01:56.844 --> 00:01:59.351
Carvajal wore a billowing,
long-sleeve shirt,

00:01:59.351 --> 00:02:02.056
cut-off slacks, and work boots.

00:02:02.056 --> 00:02:03.891
He didn't dress like a sports champion,

00:02:03.891 --> 00:02:06.391
but damn, did he run like one.

00:02:11.942 --> 00:02:12.987
With each and every step,

00:02:12.987 --> 00:02:15.332
Carvajal charged towards the finish line.

00:02:15.332 --> 00:02:16.981
Even though his legs never gave out,

00:02:16.981 --> 00:02:19.148
his grumbling stomach did.

00:02:20.359 --> 00:02:21.509
To satisfy his hunger,

00:02:21.509 --> 00:02:24.415
he ate some apples from a nearby orchard

00:02:24.415 --> 00:02:26.684
that turned out to be rotten.

00:02:26.684 --> 00:02:28.425
So he took a little recovery nap,

00:02:28.425 --> 00:02:31.722
and then proceeded to
finish in fourth place.

00:02:31.722 --> 00:02:34.346
And then, there's Thomas Hicks.

00:02:34.346 --> 00:02:35.449
The gold winner.

00:02:35.449 --> 00:02:36.773
The top dog.

00:02:36.773 --> 00:02:38.456
The champion.

00:02:38.456 --> 00:02:40.767
The barely standing living cadaver.

00:02:40.767 --> 00:02:43.112
You see, Hicks was so physically ill

00:02:43.112 --> 00:02:44.528
that he couldn't cross the finish line

00:02:44.528 --> 00:02:47.616
without the help of his trainers.

00:02:47.616 --> 00:02:48.476
Why?

00:02:48.476 --> 00:02:50.298
Well, back then, stimulants were legal,

00:02:50.298 --> 00:02:52.446
and strychnine, a type of rat poison,

00:02:52.446 --> 00:02:55.918
was thought to have
performance-enhancing properties.

00:02:55.918 --> 00:02:57.717
So during the run, his trainers gave him

00:02:57.717 --> 00:02:59.749
small doses to gain an edge.

00:02:59.749 --> 00:03:01.316
But instead of helping him,

00:03:01.316 --> 00:03:04.288
it was slowly killing him.

00:03:04.288 --> 00:03:06.471
Once Hicks was dragged
across the finish line,

00:03:06.471 --> 00:03:08.120
he wasn't in a cheery mood.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.267
Having lost eight pounds during the race,

00:03:10.267 --> 00:03:12.891
he was a hollowed-out husk of a man.

00:03:12.891 --> 00:03:15.028
The judges shrugged and
gave Hicks the gold,

00:03:15.028 --> 00:03:17.129
ending the nightmare.

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:19.915
As the crowd swooned over
Hicks's disheveled body,

00:03:19.915 --> 00:03:22.377
somewhere in the stands,
James Sullivan was probably

00:03:22.377 --> 00:03:24.780
sipping on a glass of fresh water,

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:26.394
feeling proud of putting together

00:03:26.394 --> 00:03:28.394
the worst marathon ever.

